              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Balfour Declaration (1917)</strong><br>\nIn 1917, British Foreign Minister Arthur Balfour issued a statement known as the Balfour Declaration. In this statement, he promised Britain's support for the establishment of a \"national home\" for the Jewish people in Palestine. This was a significant step in supporting the Zionist movement. However, the declaration was controversial as it did not consider the rights of the Arab population in Palestine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>UN Partition Plan (1947)</strong><br>\nIn 1947, the United Nations proposed a plan to partition Palestine into a Jewish and an Arab state. The plan was accepted by Jewish leaders but rejected by Arab leaders. The partition led to further tensions and conflicts in the region and marked the beginning of the Arab-Israeli conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of Israel (1948)</strong><br>\nOn May 14, 1948, the state of Israel was officially founded. This led to the first Arab-Israeli war, which lasted until 1949. During the war, Israel was able to significantly expand its territory. This war laid the groundwork for many subsequent conflicts in the region.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
