            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Six-Day War (1967)</strong><br>\nIn 1967, Israel launched a preemptive attack against its neighbors Egypt, Jordan, and Syria. Within six days, Israel captured large areas, including East Jerusalem, the West Bank, and the Gaza Strip. This war changed the geopolitical landscape of the Middle East and intensified tensions between Israel and the Arab states.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Yom Kippur War (1973)</strong><br>\nIn 1973, Egypt and Syria attacked Israel in an attempt to regain territories lost in the Six-Day War. Although Israel was initially unprepared, it was eventually able to repel the attackers and win the war. The war led to a reassessment of Israeli security policy and peace talks with Egypt.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Camp David Accords (1978)</strong><br>\nIn 1978, Egyptian President Anwar Sadat and Israeli Prime Minister Menachem Begin met in the USA for peace talks, resulting in the Camp David Accords. Egypt officially recognized Israel, and in return, Israel gave the Sinai Peninsula back to Egypt. This was a significant step in normalizing relations between Israel and one of its Arab neighbors.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
