          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Oslo Accords (1993)</strong><br>\nIn 1993, Israel and the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO) signed the Oslo Accords, which provided for self-governance for the Palestinians and recognition of Israel by the PLO. The accords were a significant step towards peace, but ongoing settlement activity and other points of contention have prevented a lasting solution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Intifada (2000-2005)</strong><br>\nBetween 2000 and 2005, the Second Intifada took place, a period of intense violence between Israelis and Palestinians. The unrest began following the visit of then-Israeli opposition leader Ariel Sharon to the Temple Mount. As a result of the violence, thousands of people were killed, and hopes for a peace process were dashed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gaza Conflict (2007-present)</strong><br>\nIn 2007, the Islamist movement Hamas took control of the Gaza Strip, leading to an ongoing blockade of the area by Israel. Since then, there have been several wars and countless confrontations between Israel and Hamas. The conflict demonstrates the ongoing tension and complexity of relations between Israel and the Palestinians.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
